Rule O1 (doc/scope_model.mld, sections 3.1 and 7): when odoc copies a term
from one unit into another, every root in it keeps its origin, the unit whose
source it came from. This file tests the operations that expand a term: an
alias to a hidden module, an alias to a self-canonical module, [include],
[module type of], and a module type path.

Libraries [ext1] and [ext2] each have a module [Ext], with different
contents. [x] is a dune wrapped library built against [ext1]. Its alias
module [X] is generated by dune, and its members [Hid], [Inc] and [Mt] are
compiled with [-open X]. Each member has the pure alias [E = Ext], so [x]
records no digest for [Ext] (rule P2, section 3.2). [y] is an unwrapped
library built against [ext1], with the same alias in [Ycan]. [z] is built
against [x], [y] and [ext1]. Each unit of [z] shows one of [x]'s or [y]'s
aliases by one operation.

No library here is built against [ext2], so no unit sees two [Ext]s (D3,
section 2). Under D3, [z] can only see the [Ext] that [x] and [y] see, so
these cases cannot tell the scope of [x] from the scope of [z]
(origin_store.t, case 3, can). What they can show is that the root keeps a
scope at all. [ext2] is given at link, like
every library. A root that lost its origin would be looked up among all the
libraries given at link, and would find two [Ext]s.

  $ cat x/x.ml-gen
  (** @canonical X.Hid *)
  module Hid = X__Hid
  
  (** @canonical X.Inc *)
  module Inc = X__Inc
  
  (** @canonical X.Mt *)
  module Mt = X__Mt
  $ cat x/hid.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ cat x/inc.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ cat x/mt.mli
  module E = Ext
  
  module type S = sig
    module E = Ext
  end
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -c ext1/ext.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -c ext2/ext.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -c std/std.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -w -49 -I x -I ext1 -o x/x -c -impl x/x.ml-gen
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I x -I ext1 -open X -o x/x__Hid -c -intf x/hid.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I x -I ext1 -open X -o x/x__Inc -c -intf x/inc.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I x -I ext1 -open X -o x/x__Mt -c -intf x/mt.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I y -I ext1 -c y/ycan.mli
  $ for m in hidden canon incl typeof mtpath nodoc; do
  >   ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I z -I x -I y -I std -I ext1 -c z/$m.mli
  > done

odoc compiles each unit after the units it depends on (D5).

  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/ext1 -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 ext1/ext.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/ext2 -L ext2:h/pkg/ext2 ext2/ext.cmti
  $ for u in x__Hid x__Inc x__Mt; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/x -L x:h/pkg/x -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 x/$u.cmti
  > done
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/x -L x:h/pkg/x -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 x/x.cmt
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/y -L y:h/pkg/y -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 y/ycan.cmti
  $ for m in hidden canon incl typeof mtpath; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/z -L z:h/pkg/z -L x:h/pkg/x -L y:h/pkg/y -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 z/$m.cmti
  > done

  $ link() { odoc link -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 -L ext2:h/pkg/ext2 -L x:h/pkg/x -L y:h/pkg/y -L z:h/pkg/z "$@"; }
  $ show() { odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents --show-expansions --show-include-expansions "$@"; }

How to read [odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents]. Each line
is one item of the signature. [x/0] is the item [x], with a number odoc adds
to tell apart items of the same name. [resolved(...)] is a resolved path, and
[root(pkg/ext1/Ext)] is the unit [Ext] of the library [ext1] in the package
[pkg]. [unresolvedroot(Ext)] is a root that is not resolved yet. [hidden(...)]
marks a path to a hidden unit, and [canonical(p,resolved(q))] a path [p]
whose canonical path is [q]. [r(...)] is a resolved module type path. With
[--show-expansions] and [--show-include-expansions], [=>] is followed by the
signature odoc computed for a module or an [include].

Case 1. Expand an alias to a hidden module. [Hidden] has [M = X__Hid], which
names the hidden unit itself. The link shows [X__Hid]'s expansion on
[Hidden]'s page. Its [E = Ext] was written in [x], so [E] should be [ext1]'s
[Ext], with no warning:

  $ cat z/hidden.mli
  module M = X__Hid
  $ cat ext1/ext.mli
  type t = A
  $ cat ext2/ext.mli
  type t = B
  $ link h/pkg/z/hidden.odoc
  File "Ext":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/ext1/Ext, pkg/ext2/Ext
  $ show h/pkg/z/hidden.odocl
  module M/0 = resolved(hidden(root(pkg/x/X__Hid)))
    => sig module E/1 = resolved(root(pkg/ext2/Ext)) end

Case 2. Expand an alias to a self-canonical module. [Ycan] has the canonical
path [Canon.M], and [Canon] has [M = Ycan], so the link shows [Ycan]'s
expansion on [Canon]'s page, as eio does for its modules. Its [E = Ext] was
written in [y], so [E] should be [ext1]'s [Ext], with no warning:

  $ cat z/canon.mli
  module M = Ycan
  $ cat y/ycan.mli
  (** @canonical Canon.M *)
  
  module E = Ext
  $ link h/pkg/z/canon.odoc
  File "Ext":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/ext1/Ext, pkg/ext2/Ext
  $ show h/pkg/z/canon.odocl
  module M/0 =
    resolved(canonical(root(pkg/y/Ycan),resolved(root(pkg/z/Canon).M)))
    => sig module E/1 = resolved(root(pkg/ext2/Ext)) end

Case 3. Recompute an [include]. [Incl] has [include module type of X.Inc].
odoc recomputes the signature from [x]'s unit, so the [Ext] in it has the
origin [X__Inc] (section 3.1). [Incl] itself should have no entry for [Ext]
in its import table (section 1). [entry FILE N], from [../digests.sh],
prints [digest], [no digest] or [no entry] for [N] in [FILE]'s import
table:

  $ cat z/incl.mli
  include module type of X.Inc
  $ . ../digests.sh
  $ entry z/incl.cmi Ext
  no entry

So [Incl]'s table cannot give the root a meaning. [E] should be [ext1]'s [Ext], with
no warning:

  $ link h/pkg/z/incl.odoc
  File "Ext":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/ext1/Ext, pkg/ext2/Ext
  $ show h/pkg/z/incl.odocl
  include module type of resolved(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Inc,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Inc)))
    => sig module E/0 = resolved(root(pkg/ext2/Ext)) end

Case 4. Recompute [module type of]. [Typeof] has [M : module type of X.Mt]. The
[Ext]s in [M]'s signature, in a module and in a module type, have the origin
[X__Mt]. Each should be [ext1]'s [Ext], with no warning:

  $ cat z/typeof.mli
  module M : module type of X.Mt
  $ link h/pkg/z/typeof.odoc
  File "Ext":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/ext1/Ext, pkg/ext2/Ext
  File "Ext":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/ext1/Ext, pkg/ext2/Ext
  $ show h/pkg/z/typeof.odocl
  module M/0 : module type of resolved(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Mt,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Mt)))
    => sig
      module E/1 = resolved(root(pkg/ext2/Ext))
      module type S/2 = sig module E/3 = resolved(root(pkg/ext2/Ext)) end
     end

Case 5. Expand a module type path. [Mtpath] has [M : X.Mt.S]. odoc expands
[X.Mt.S] from [x]'s unit, so the [Ext] in [M]'s expansion has the origin
[X__Mt]. It should be [ext1]'s [Ext], with no warning:

  $ cat z/mtpath.mli
  module M : X.Mt.S
  $ link h/pkg/z/mtpath.odoc
  File "Ext":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/ext1/Ext, pkg/ext2/Ext
  $ show h/pkg/z/mtpath.odocl
  module M/0 : r(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Mt,resolved(root(pkg/x/X).Mt)).S)
    => sig module E/1 = resolved(root(pkg/ext2/Ext)) end

Case 6. A recomputation that fails. Library [std] stands for the standard
library, which dune does not document: [Nodoc] is built against it with
[-I], but no [-L] names it. This breaks D1. [Nodoc] has [include module type
of Std]. odoc cannot find [Std], so it cannot recompute the signature. It
should keep the compiler's signature, which is outside the claims of section
3.7 (section 3.1). The innermost root that fails, [Std], is missing, so the
failure should be reported only with [--enable-missing-root-warning]
(section 3.6):

  $ cat z/nodoc.mli
  include module type of Std
  $ cat std/std.mli
  type t = S
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/z -L z:h/pkg/z -L x:h/pkg/x -L y:h/pkg/y -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 z/nodoc.cmti
  $ odoc compile --enable-missing-root-warning --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/z -L z:h/pkg/z -L x:h/pkg/x -L y:h/pkg/y -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 z/nodoc.cmti
  File "z/nodoc.cmti":
  Warning: Couldn't find the following modules:
    Std
  $ show h/pkg/z/nodoc.odoc
  include module type of unresolvedroot(Std) => sig type t/0 = S end
